==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɹæmp ˈʌp/<qq:in isolation>|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Aadenboy-ramp up.wav|a=US}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|*}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[increase]] rapidly to a new value.
#: {{nearsyn|en|build up}}
#: {{ux|en|Headcount '''ramped up''' over three months.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[increase]] (something) rapidly to a new value; to cause (something) to ramp up.
#: {{nearsyn|en|build up}}
#: {{ux|en|The manager '''ramped up''' headcount.}}
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|date=November 20, 2012|author=Nina Bernstein|title=Storm Bared a Lack of Options for the Homeless in New York|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/21/nyregion/storm-worsens-shortage-of-nyc-housing-for-homeless.html?ref=nyregion
|passage=Overnight, as the storm bore down on urban flood zones, city officials '''ramped up''' emergency spaces to shelter thousands more people, mostly in public schools and colleges.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250624054223/https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/21/nyregion/storm-worsens-shortage-of-nyc-housing-for-homeless.html?ref=nyregion|archivedate=24 June 2025}}
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|date=2021-06-22|author=Nicholas Fandos|title=Republicans Block Voting Rights Bill, Dealing Blow to Biden and Democrats|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/06/22/us/politics/filibuster-voting-rights.html|passage=Liberal activists promised a well-funded summertime blitz, replete with home-state rallies and million-dollar ad campaigns, to try to '''ramp up''' pressure on a handful of Senate Democrats opposed to changing the rules.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260109215839/https://www.nytimes.com/2021/06/22/us/politics/filibuster-voting-rights.html|archivedate=9 January 2026}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|date=March 13, 2023|author=Eric Cheung; Nectar Gan|title=Taiwan says soldier who went missing has been found in China|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230313102219/https://www.cnn.com/2023/03/13/asia/taiwan-missing-soldier-found-china-intl-hnk/index.html|archivedate=13 March 2023|text=In recent years, Beijing has '''ramped up''' economic, diplomatic and military pressure on Taiwan – a self-ruling democracy the Chinese Communist Party claims as its own despite having never governed it.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[start up]]; to be in an initial phase of rapid increase.
#: {{nearsyn|en|fire up|spin up}}
#: {{ux|en|The 5G development project will '''ramp up''' in the first quarter.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Jana Murphy|title=The Secret Lives of Toddlers|publisher=Perigee|isbn=978-0-399-53023-4|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=jWKLma9osEIC&pg=PA118&dq=ramping-up|page=118
|passage=It's only fair to note that during the toddler years, the nightmare phase is just '''ramping up''' for many children.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=John A. Cogliandro|title=Intelligent Innovation: Four Steps to Achieving a Competitive Edge|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=3VRs0vB25PoC&pg=PA71&dq=ramping-up|page=71|publisher=J. Ross Publishing|year_published=2007|isbn=978-1-932159-61-5
|passage=For projects that are just '''ramping up''', a reasonable estimate should be made for the foreseeable future.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|management}} To start (something) up; to cause (something) to ramp up.
#: {{nearsyn|en|fire up|spin up}}
#: {{ux|en|He wants to be first to '''ramp up''' his project.}}

